<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 40 "Feel Flow" M2 — the LANE HEATMAP, the pixel gate.

  A track-level groove analysis (M1/M1b: STrack::startFeelFlowBounce()) shows
  up on the arranger as a bottom band on the track's OWN lane, tinted per
  column from the "groove.res" compliance scalar. This is the AC 4 case:
  false (never analyzed) then analyze then true (with a minPixels floor and
  the colour relations, plus a PNG grab) then a staling edit then false again.

  THE SLOT CORRECTION (kickoff AC 2). The design's v2 text said "right after
  drawChildSumOverlay", i.e. BEHIND the clip loop, which paints the band
  invisible on exactly the clip-covered lanes this feature targets (a clip
  fills its rect with QColor(160,160,160) and would paint straight over
  anything drawn earlier). The band is instead drawn LAST, after every clip,
  as a ~20%-of-lane-height strip along the bottom
  (STrackRendererInline::drawFeelFlowBand). This case's single track carries
  ONE clip spanning nearly the whole visible width, so it is the one
  configuration that would have caught the wrong slot outright: an
  expectOverlay="true" assertion over a lane covered edge-to-edge by a clip
  body PASSES on the corrected code and would have measured 0 overlay pixels
  on the v2 slot.

  THE COLOUR LAW, UPDATED 2026-08-21 (aggressive visibility follow-up). M2's
  original band interpolated between the lane fill and the clip body grey at
  partial alpha — the requester found it too subtle ("rainbow, in case I miss
  shades of grey"). The band now paints OPAQUE from ONE authoritative LUT,
  STrackRendererInline::feelFlowPalette(): a quantized 24-step hue ramp, RED
  (low compliance, hue 0 deg) through YELLOW to GREEN (high compliance,
  hue 120 deg), full saturation, value ~0.85 — traffic-light semantics, red is
  the hot spot to edit/listen/overdub. The old luminance-relation gate
  (strictly lighter than the fill, strictly darker than the clip body) still
  runs unmodified below (it is now a WEAKER, informational check — the
  palette's yellows and greens still land inside that band, but nothing about
  the relation is load-bearing for this feature any more); the STRONGER,
  load-bearing gate is exact-RGB LUT membership: `lutPixels=` counts pixels
  whose exact colour is one of the 24 palette entries, and
  `lutIndexMin=`/`lutIndexMax=` report the observed compliance range. Opaque
  paint makes an exact match sound — no compositing, no partial alpha, no
  noise floor to name.

  THE HOP DOMAIN (AC 2's "placement arithmetic only, no warp map"). The
  bounce is a whole-project render starting at project frame 0
  (sfeelflowbounce.cpp), so a "groove.res" hop's frame position IS a
  track-timeline frame, and STrackRendererInline::drawFeelFlowBand divides
  ctx.getTimeOf(x) by hopFrames directly, no clip-relative offset, no
  sourceToWarpedExact, anywhere in the paint path. The fixture
  (../groove/a_offset15.wav, the same one feel_flow_bounce_stale.qxa uses)
  is ~778452 frames (~16.2 s @ 48 kHz); the default arranger zoom is
  30 px/second (SStdMixerView::secondWidth_), so a 900 px grab shows a 30 s
  window and the whole clip, plus material AFTER the clip's own extent,
  which is exactly where hop >= nHops and the band correctly stops.

  THE STALING EDIT is set-track-volume, deliberately NOT the insert-plugin
  feel_flow_bounce_stale.qxa uses: a second, independent path to the same
  content-epoch bump (STrack::onTrackVolumeChanged -> invalidateRenderPath(),
  which is what feeds twGainStage, downstream of which sits the root
  component the bounce reads and STrack::feelFlowStale() checks).

  maxPixels vs maxLutPixels — WHY BOTH, AND WHY ONLY ONE IS LOAD-BEARING NOW.
  `maxPixels` (kept, unchanged since M2) names a NOISE FLOOR: measured on this
  exact fixture, a freshly-added, never-analyzed clip already contributes
  ~1100-1200 pixels in the "strictly between fill and clip body" luminance
  range, EVEN with bandOnly="true" scoping the scan to the bottom fifth of the
  lane (a waveform's troughs reach the bottom of the clip rect as often as the
  top) — the clip's own waveform paint, not a defect in the feel-flow band,
  and unchanged by staling (byte-identical in the log before the first
  analysis and after the staling edit). It stays as a legacy/informational
  check. `maxLutPixels="0"` (2026-08-21) is the load-bearing negative
  assertion now: since the band paints fully opaque from a fixed 24-entry LUT
  and nothing else in this project ever paints one of those exact colours, an
  absent-or-stale band is measured as EXACTLY ZERO LUT pixels — no floor, no
  margin, no noise to name. Measured on this fixture: lutPixels=0 both before
  the first analysis and after the staling edit.

  minPixels vs minLutPixels/minLutSpread — the FRESH case. `minPixels="2500"`
  (kept) is the old luminance-relation floor. `minLutPixels` (2026-08-21) is
  the load-bearing floor on exact LUT membership: measured 9234 lutPixels on
  this fixture (a wider count than the old 8779 overlayPixels, because opaque
  palette colours land squarely inside the same luminance band the old
  metric also counted, plus a few palette entries the interpolated tint never
  reached) — 8000 leaves ~1200 of margin below the measurement.
  `minLutSpread="10"` asserts the ramp is not flat: this fixture's compliance
  DOES vary (verified, not assumed) — the observed range is the full
  lutIndexMin=0 .. lutIndexMax=23, i.e. every one of the 24 steps appears
  somewhere in the band, so 10 is a wide-margin floor under a measured spread
  of 23, proving "at least 2 distinct palette indices appear" many times
  over (this is an XML comment, so no literal double hyphen above).
  assert-groove-aspect's `stale=` attribute gates the exact boolean
  (STrack::feelFlowStale()) the paint code branches on.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="feel_flow_heatmap">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- The grid crosses every lane and would land in the pixel histogram as
         chrome; the overlay gate wants a clean band (proposal 39's own
         convention for assert-lane-overlay). -->
    <grid-disable/>
    <sidecar-root/>

    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <!-- Every pixel count below (the ~1131px noise floor, minLutPixels="8000")
         is measured over a lane band of the 100px base height this case was
         written at, and scales with it - so PIN it rather than inherit the
         view default, which is a UI preference and has moved once already
         (100 -> 49, which halves the band and every count in it). -->
    <set-lane-view trackHeight="100"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../groove/a_offset15.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- ============ AC 4 (1/3): never analyzed -> NOTHING paints ========= -->
    <!-- STrack::feelFlowStale() is true (no bounce ever completed, the safe
         answer per sfeelflowbounce.h's own doc) whether or not a holder even
         exists yet, so the band contributes NOTHING beyond the clip's own
         intrinsic waveform noise (measured ~1131 px, see the header note);
         maxPixels names that floor with margin (legacy/informational).
         maxLutPixels="0" is the LOAD-BEARING check since 2026-08-21: the
         opaque band never painted, so EXACT zero LUT pixels, no margin. -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         expectOverlay="false" bandOnly="true" maxPixels="2500"
                         maxLutPixels="0"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="1"
                          expectExists="false"/>

    <feel-flow-analyze target="track" trackPath="0"/>
    <wait-analysis timeoutMs="60000"/>

    <!-- Sanity: the analysis actually produced data before asking the paint
         path to show it (fresh, in-bounds record count, feel_flow_bounce_
         stale.qxa's own bracket). -->
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="0"
                          expectExists="true" minRecords="1500" maxRecords="1650"/>

    <!-- ========= AC 4 (2/3): fresh -> the band paints, in-relation ======= -->
    <!-- Only wait-analysis stands between the analyze and this assertion
         (AC 3: repaint rides captureRevalidated() with no manual poke, the
         same notifyCaptureRevalidated() the analysis job's completion
         already queues for the badge). minPixels=2500 sits ABOVE the
         measured noise floor's ceiling (legacy/informational). Since
         2026-08-21 the LOAD-BEARING assertions are minLutPixels="8000"
         (measured 9234, ~1200 margin) and minLutSpread="10" (measured
         lutIndexMin=0..lutIndexMax=23, i.e. every one of the 24 palette
         steps appears; the fixture's compliance genuinely varies). -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         minPixels="2500" bandOnly="true"
                         minLutPixels="8000" minLutSpread="10"
                         grabPng="feel_flow_heatmap.png"/>

    <!-- =========== AC 4 (3/3): a staling edit -> NOTHING again =========== -->
    <set-track-volume trackPath="0" volume="-6"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="1"
                          expectExists="true"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         expectOverlay="false" bandOnly="true" maxPixels="2500"
                         maxLutPixels="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
